verdicts.changes:
Keep the closed forms. That covers:
- camera x on the subject's edge;
- eye = floor + 1.7;
- yaw from the wanted meeting point;
- d_max from the 0.70 reach;
- eye height for overview views;
- L_max at the nearest painted surface in the end frame;
- plate lens from corner rays;
- the people limits (backs to the camera at 6 m or more from every camera position along the slide; the crowd's long axis at least 60 deg to every view);
- prop turns from a stored front axis.

Add shot-kind profiles with their exceptions written down: still life, crowd, overview, interior, dark subject. Examples: a crowd's box limit is set by the crowd; an overview lets a fill prop reach 0.70; an opening as subject fails rule 1 vertically. Give a rule order for conflicts, such as reach 0.70 against nothing big at bottom left.

Drop the silhouette fallback. Facing figures get the 'face lost in shadow' prompt clause and an after-painting skin-pixel check, and the 24 px figure is marked 'calibrate on the first Works painting'. Crowd spacing should be head centres at least one head width apart with every figure at least 50% visible, not rule 4's 15/18 px.

Drop the hull and cylinder rule predictions. Run shot_facts in Workbench (about 15 s) plus check_shot instead, auto-apply camera-tier fixes only, and report the rest. Validate on checkout C1, a place not used to derive these rules. This brings the effort down from large to medium.

verdicts.problems:
Right physics: a Blender spot of P watts lights its target like a point light of P watts, with the cone only masking, so dropping the inventory's cone formula was correct.

The tuner's goals fight on the Works:
- In round 5 final W1 the gate is 0.85x the frame mean, warm light covers 0.2%, the brazier reaches 0.24 against the gate's 0.11, and every rim-lit man outshines the gate.
- Raising the fire for rule 6 makes rule 5 worse. Bisection per light will oscillate.
- 'subject_dark' is the design's own invention. Round 5 aimed to make the gate the brightest thing and failed, so this is a decision for the user or critic, not a fact.

Most of the 26 notice-corner rounds changed things other than watts:
- light position;
- linking;
- colour;
- coal height;
- soot liner;
- chair paint;
- board darkness.

Other numbers that miss:
- The exposure target '45-55 after the grade' would clamp W1 at 1.8. The hand value 1.6 gave 28 levels, which was accepted (page-3.js LOOK_W1).
- Plate minutes = 4 + 6*lights/3 gives 10 min for three shadow lights; round 3 measured 6.
- Default linking ('skip anything within 0.2 m') would not produce the round 5 link 'fire skips the works wall'.
- The three.js paper-stage items are old-look constants, not calculations.

verdicts.changes:
Make plan_lights the first deliverable:
- start watts by inverse square from a reference light;
- reach by ray cast, stopping short of the first surface that isn't a target;
- cone from the subject's size;
- default links: skip its own housing, and add any camera-facing non-story surface it lights brighter than the subject;
- shadow-caster list and foot patches;
- EEVEE shadow settings, and a render-log check that refuses the picture on a fault.

Make tune_lights propose one change to one named light per round, at most 8 small renders, with each rule set chosen by the subject kind. Leave material and position changes to a person.

Set exposure from the other views' on-screen mean, measured on the painting, not the plate. Keep the paper-stage numbers as constants. Drop the plate-minutes formula: measure and record instead.
